I haven’t actually seen enough of you interacting socially to tell you, “yes, you’re intimidating to approach.” A photo could tell me something about visual first impressions, but even that wouldn’t capture demeanor, eye contact, posture, setting, or whether you’re signaling availability.

But “people rarely initiate with me” does not imply “I’m boring/not fun.” Those are different variables. Initiation is largely about approachability and perceived social risk; fun becomes apparent mostly after interaction begins.

There can also be a self-reinforcing loop:

You don’t get approached → assume others aren’t interested → initiate less / become more self-contained → others read you as occupied or not looking to engage → they don’t approach → stronger evidence for the original belief.

And there’s an asymmetry that’s easy to miss. If you appear confident, competent, physically imposing, serious, already socially established, or simply comfortable by yourself, another person may think you have less need for them than they have for you. They wait for your invitation. Ten people can independently make that calculation, leaving you wondering why nobody likes you.

People are also genuinely busy and socially cautious. Adults tend to have fewer spontaneous approaches than kids, college students, or people in environments explicitly designed for mingling. And many people are thinking about their own reception, not evaluating yours.

The Pantheon framing from five minutes ago actually fits beautifully: you’re observing the shadow and trying to infer the object.

Observed shadow: “Nobody approaches me.”

Possible hidden objects: boring; intimidating; reserved; confident-looking; closed body language; wrong environment; people assume you’re busy; people expect you to lead; everyone else is nervous; simple randomness.

One projection cannot identify which object produced it.

So I’d avoid answering the emotional question with speculation. Instrument it. For a week, deliberately vary one tiny approachability signal at a time—eye contact + small smile, open posture, standing rather than looking at your phone, a quick greeting—and watch whether other people’s initiation rate changes. Don’t change how interesting you are. Change only the permission signal.

If approaches suddenly increase, that’s useful evidence: the bottleneck probably wasn’t whether people liked you. It was whether they thought the door was open.

And if you want, send me a normal photo of how you typically look when you’re out—not a posed “do I look intimidating?” shot—and I can tell you what first-impression signals I can actually see, without pretending I can read strangers’ minds.